<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<odoo>
    <!--
        =====================================================================
        A default DMS access group, so a fresh installation can create a
        folder at all.
        =====================================================================

        THE PROBLEM
        ===========
        OCA `dms` grants the right to create a directory or a file ONLY to
        users who are in a `dms.access.group` attached to that directory:

            dms/models/dms_security_mixin.py, _get_access_groups_query():
                SELECT dir_group_rel.aid
                FROM dms_directory_complete_groups_rel ...
                WHERE users.uid = %s AND dag.perm_inclusive_create

        A fresh `dms` installation has ZERO access groups. Its onboarding
        (`dms/data/onboarding_data.xml`) walks the user through Storage ->
        Directory -> File and never mentions access groups. So the first
        thing anyone does — create a folder — fails with:

            Uh-oh! ... Administrator (id=2) doesn't have 'create' access to
            - Directory (dms.directory)

        Verified 2026-11-05 on `luke18`: after fixing the record-rule bug
        (see `dms_rule_workaround.xml`) the error remained, because
        `SELECT count(*) FROM dms_access_group` was 0.

        WHAT THIS DOES
        ==============
        Creates one group, "Administrators", with full permissions and every
        `base.group_system` member in it. That is enough for an administrator
        to create the first root directory; from there, normal DMS practice
        applies — a directory's `group_ids` decides who may write in it, and
        subdirectories inherit.

        ⚠️ This is NOT a blanket grant. It does not give ordinary internal
        users write access to DMS. It only makes sure the person who
        administers the system can get started, instead of hitting a dead end
        with no hint about which group is missing.

        ⚠️ `noupdate="1"`: an upgrade must not resurrect the group if an
        administrator renamed it, changed its members, or deleted it on
        purpose. The seed exists to bootstrap a NEW installation.
    -->
    <data noupdate="1">
        <record id="access_group_administrators" model="dms.access.group">
            <field name="name">Administrators</field>
            <field name="perm_create" eval="True" />
            <field name="perm_write" eval="True" />
            <field name="perm_unlink" eval="True" />
            <field
                name="group_ids"
                eval="[(4, ref('base.group_system'))]"
            />
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
